## The problem
In many coding interviews you are asked to implement a linked list from scratch. The interviewer wants to see that you understand pointer manipulation, edge‑case handling (empty list, single element, tail updates) and can write clean, reusable JavaScript code. A singly linked list is the simplest linear structure, while a doubly linked list adds a back‑pointer, enabling O(1) deletions from both ends.

## How it works
A **Node** stores a value and a reference to the next node. In a doubly linked list it also stores a reference to the previous node. The **LinkedList** class keeps a reference to the head (first node) and, for convenience, a tail (last node). Core operations:
- **add(value)** – creates a new node and attaches it after the current tail; updates head if the list was empty.
- **remove(value)** – walks the list, relinks neighbours when the target is found, and updates head/tail when needed.
- **find(value)** – linear scan returning the node or `null`.
- **toArray()** – useful for testing; walks from head to tail collecting values.
For a doubly linked list the removal step also rewires the `prev` pointer of the successor. All operations run in O(n) time except adding to the tail, which is O(1) because we keep a tail reference.

## Example
```js
// Singly linked list implementation
class Node {
  constructor(value) {
    this.value = value;
    this.next = null;
  }
}

class LinkedList {
  constructor() {
    this.head = null;
    this.tail = null;
  }

  add(value) {
    const node = new Node(value);
    if (!this.head) {
      this.head = this.tail = node;
    } else {
      this.tail.next = node;
      this.tail = node;
    }
  }

  remove(value) {
    if (!this.head) return;
    while (this.head && this.head.value === value) {
      this.head = this.head.next;
    }
    let cur = this.head;
    while (cur && cur.next) {
      if (cur.next.value === value) {
        cur.next = cur.next.next;
        if (cur.next === null) this.tail = cur;
      } else {
        cur = cur.next;
      }
    }
    if (this.head === null) this.tail = null;
  }

  find(value) {
    let cur = this.head;
    while (cur) {
      if (cur.value === value) return cur;
      cur = cur.next;
    }
    return null;
  }

  toArray() {
    const arr = [];
    let cur = this.head;
    while (cur) {
      arr.push(cur.value);
      cur = cur.next;
    }
    return arr;
  }
}

// Demo
const list = new LinkedList();
list.add(1);
list.add(2);
list.add(3);
list.remove(2);
console.log(list.toArray()); // [1, 3]
```
The output comment shows the expected result after removing the middle element.

## Trade-offs and pitfalls
- Forgetting to update `tail` after removal leads to stale references and O(n) tail inserts.
- Removing from the head without a loop can leave the list empty but still point `tail` to an old node.
- In a doubly linked list, neglecting to set `prev` on the new head or tail breaks backward traversal.
- Using `===` for value comparison works for primitives; for objects you need a custom comparator.
- Mutating the list while iterating (e.g., removing the current node) requires careful pointer updates to avoid skipping nodes.

## In the interview
**What is asked?** Implement a singly (or doubly) linked list with `add`, `remove`, `find`, and a way to visualize the contents. The interviewer may also ask about time/space complexity.
**Strong short answer:** "I keep `head` and `tail` pointers, each `add` appends in O(1), `remove` and `find` walk the list in O(n). For a doubly linked list I also maintain a `prev` pointer, which makes deletions O(1) once the node is located."
**Common follow‑ups:**
- How would you implement `remove` in O(1) without a previous pointer? (Answer: keep a map from value to node or use a sentinel node.)
- How to reverse the list in‑place? (Swap `next` pointers while traversing.)
- How does memory usage compare to an array? (Linked list uses extra node objects, so higher overhead.)
- Can you make the list iterable? (Implement `[Symbol.iterator]` that yields each value.)

